<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="abscisic acid degradation by glucosylation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5272" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The plant hormone abscisic acid (ABA) is involved in the regulation of several crucial physiological and developmental processes in plants, as well as in the adaptative responses to environmental stresses |CITS:[zeevaart88]|. The regulation of these processes hinges on the size of the bioactive ABA pool. This pool is thought to be maintained through a finely tuned equilibrium between ABA biosynthesis and catabolism. Among the ABA catabolic pathways, the 8&apos;-hydroxylation pathway leading to the formation of phaseic acid (PA) appears to be the major regulation point. In addition, the catabolic sequence of the formation of |FRAME:CPD-7729| via 9&apos;-hydroxylation of |FRAME:CPD-693| (see |FRAME:PWY-7640|) and the formation of |FRAME:CPD-17688| (see |FRAME:PWY-7642|) are also considered to be involved in the cellular regulation of this phytohormone. Another important ABA catabolic route operates through the conjugation of ABA (or its hydroxylated derivatives such as PA) with glucose. One of the most widespread conjugates is |FRAME:CPD-7727| (ABA-GE) |CITS:[16662451]|. ABA conjugates are thought to be physiologically inactive and to accumulate in vacuoles as the plant matures |CITS:[16664479],[lehmann86]|. Recently, however, ABA-GE was postulated to be involved in the long-distance transport of ABA through the vascular system |CITS:[11741037],[hansen99]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The glucosylation of ABA to form ABA-GE has been shown to be catalyzed by a number of glucosyltransferases from Adzuki bean and &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[12114582],[lim05]|. These enzymes require UDP-glucose as substrate for the reaction. They tend to be able to use a variety of substrates such as indole-3-acetate or &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-cinnamate. The existence of a &amp;beta;-glucosidase able to catalyze the release of ABA from ABA-GE has been reported in crude extracts of barley leaves, more specifically in the intercellular washing fluid |CITS:[10948220]|. The anabolic reaction resulting in |FRAME:CPD-7727| (ABA-GE) catalyzed by various abscisic acid glucosyltransferases and the catabolic sequence releasing |FRAME:CPD-693| from the ABA-GE by abscisic acid glucose ester &amp;beta;-glucosidases appears to be fine-tuned to the localized and overall cellular regulation of |FRAME:CPD-693|. In contrast to the 7&apos;-, 8&apos;- and 9&apos;-hydroxylation pathways (see |FRAME:PWY-7642|, |FRAME:PWY-5271| and |FRAME:PWY-7640|, respectively) which irreversibly degrade |FRAME:CPD-693|, the metabolism of |FRAME:CPD-7727| reflects an pathway which can quickly remove or add |FRAME:CPD-693| in response to the respective requirements of the cell. The interaction of anabolic and catabolic enzymes in the pathway to contribute to the fine-tuning of |FRAME:CPD-693| in the cell has been demonstrated. The |FRAME:AT3G21780-MONOMER| together with two homolog UDP-glucosyltransferases is involved in the formation of |FRAME:CPD-7727|, hence lower the cellular |FRAME:CPD-693| concentration and shown, when knocked down by RNAi in transgenic &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; plants, to affect |FRAME:CPD-693| responses by interfering with the expression of genes involved in the 8&apos;-hydroxylation pathway (see |FRAME:PWY-5271|). This expression-repression pattern is inversed, i.e. when the glucosyltransferase(s) are suppressed the expression of the 8&apos;-hydroxylation pathway (Cyp707A1 - A4) is induced and vice versa |CITS:[24676855]|. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that the CYP707A enzymes are also involved in the 9&apos;-hydroxylation of |FRAME:CPD-693|, hence the |FRAME:PWY-7640| is also directly affected by the functional status of the glucosyltransferase(s) |CITS:[21414645]|. Along the same line of experimental evidence it has been demonstrated that the abscisic acid glucose ester &amp;beta;-glucosidases in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; encoded by BG1 |CITS:[16990135]| and BG2 |CITS:[22582100]| releasing |FRAME:CPD-693| from |FRAME:CPD-7727| are also in sync with the expression behavior of the glucosyltransferase(s) |CITS:[24676855]|. Interestingly, the &amp;beta;-glucosidases are located in different cell organelles, BG1 is localized to the endoplasmatic reticulum (ER) whereas BG2 is situated in the vacuole. The fact that BG2 can rescue &amp;Delta;bg1 mutant plants indicates the existence of an regulatory network for the homeostasis of |FRAME:CPD-693| across cellular organelles, which along with the glucosyltransferase(s) located in the cytosol provide a net of |FRAME:CPD-693| receptors which may be involved in adjusting the required concentration of |FRAME:CPD-693| more locally rather than on an overall cellular level by coordinating catabolic and anabolic pathways of |FRAME:CPD-693| |CITS:[24676855] [22582100]|.</Description>
